Subject: [PATCH 0/2] mm: batch file VMA insertion and defer page table copying

This series batches file VMA insertion during fork. Each batch links up to
eight VMAs for one address_space under one i_mmap write lock, then copies
their page tables after releasing the mapping and dcache locks.

Deferring copies permits adjacent COW VMAs to share an insertion batch.
The batch entry still processes individual VMAs through copy_page_range;
MMU notifiers, write-protect sequencing and the page-table copy semantics
are unchanged. Error paths link all installed VMAs before teardown, even
when an earlier member of the batch failed to copy.

The local v7 revision keeps the common vma_file_batch layout introduced
while following 3577dbb19241 ("mm: batch unlink_file_vma calls in
free_pgd_range"). The source/destination pair buffer remains 136 bytes on
x86_64. The second patch adds fork/truncate/sealing coverage, including
all-COW batches and alternating files that need copying.

Validation used an x86_64 QEMU guest with KASAN and lockdep: 14 fork_file
selftests passed, guard tests passed 5 with 4 skips, SysV SHM lifetime
checks passed, and 64 overcommit-induced fork failures were cleaned up.
A project diagnostic tested 84 allocation-injection cases in
copy_page_range, with 54 ENOMEM failures followed by successful recovery
forks and write-seal checks. Traces confirmed insertion of the whole
batch before copying and covered errors at capacity, mapping changes,
the tail and an anonymous copy with pending file VMAs.

With the same UnixBench spawn binaries used for the previous local
revision, 3481 observed forks at one and four workers changed from two
to one insertion batch for the static executable, and from six to three
for the dynamically linked executable. Each batch held five VMAs.
These are batching measurements, not hardware throughput results.

The implementation and tests were developed with LLM assistance. The
fault-injection and tracing diagnostics are project artifacts, not part
of the upstream selftest patch. UFFD, hugetlb and device-specific paths
were not exercised at runtime in this revision.

